\chapter{Paper, Light, and Little Languages}
\label{ch:typesetting}
\index{C/A/T phototypesetter}\index{troff}\index{Ossanna, Joseph}\index{document preparation}

\section{The printed page was a computing problem}

A patent application or mathematical paper is more than a sequence of
letters. It contains hierarchy, symbols, alignment, references, and layout
constraints. An editor lets an author change the sequence; a formatter
interprets that sequence as instructions for a page. Unix's early success
in document preparation joined these two activities without pretending
they were identical.

This separation had practical consequences. The same source could be
revised, searched, compared, stored on tape, and formatted repeatedly.
An author could change a paragraph without retyping an entire page.
Formatting rules could be improved in a program or macro package rather
than enforced through repeated manual correction. The page became the
output of a computation whose inputs remained available for further work.

The distinction is familiar in modern publishing, where documents may be
built from Markdown, XML, or LaTeX. It was already useful on hardware whose
most common output device resembled a typewriter. Unix did not invent every
part of this tradition. Its formatters descended through earlier systems,
including runoff and roff. What it supplied was a particularly fertile
environment for combining editors, formatters, preprocessors, and devices
\parencite{evolution,roddy}.

\section{What the C/A/T actually did}

In 1973 Joe Ossanna arranged for a Graphic Systems CAT phototypesetter at
Bell Labs. The machine commonly styled C/A/T exposed character images onto
photographic paper. Fonts existed as physical images on film mounted in a
rotating mechanism. Selecting a character, positioning it, changing size,
and exposing the image involved hardware with mechanical limitations.
It was not a laser printer waiting to be driven by a convenient page
language.

Kernighan's memoir describes four fonts of 102 characters on the wheel and
sixteen available sizes. The photographic output then required chemical
processing. These details matter because they explain why typography could
shape software structure. A formatter had to know enough about widths and
positioning to produce attractive output while respecting what the device
could actually do. High quality did not imply easy operation
\parencite[discussion of the Graphic Systems CAT]{kernighan-history}.

Ossanna's troff turned that device into a publishing resource. Its name
identified a typesetter-oriented member of the formatter family; nroff
served terminal-like output. The two shared much of their input language,
allowing a document to be proofread cheaply and then typeset. The common
source was valuable even when the proof could not reproduce every
mathematical symbol or spacing decision exactly.

The C/A/T therefore belongs in a history of Unix for more than picturesque
reasons. It connected the operating system to a task whose result could be
judged by people who did not care how the kernel was organized. A cleanly
printed paper was visible evidence that the tools worked. Better output
also raised expectations and motivated better tools.

\section{Requests, text, and macro packages}
\index{macros!troff}

A roff-family input file mixes text with requests. A line beginning with a
control character can select a font, set spacing, or invoke a macro.
The formatter reads an instruction stream, maintains state, and emits
positioned material. It is useful to think of this as an interpreter rather
than as a typewriter with unusually complicated keys.

Raw formatting requests expose too many details for ordinary authors.
A macro package provides a higher-level vocabulary for paragraphs,
headings, titles, lists, and notes. Consider this newly written fragment
in the traditional \texttt{ms} style:

\begin{verbatim}
.TL
A Small System for a Large Task
.AU
An Example Author
.SH
The document as a program
.PP
The source describes a paragraph.
The formatter chooses the line breaks.
\end{verbatim}

The author identifies document roles rather than manually placing every
line. A macro package can change the visual treatment of a heading across
an entire document. Cherry and Lesk's account of the \texttt{ms} and
\texttt{mcs} packages shows how this vocabulary also accommodated equations,
tables, cover sheets, and institutional styles. Macro naming had to preserve
compatibility: a document corpus already depending on old commands was a
real constraint on improvement \parencite{cherry-lesk}.

There is an enduring design tension here. A rich macro language permits
local customization, but too much state and too many implicit conventions
make documents difficult to reason about. A formatter may accept valid
input that nevertheless produces a bad page. Program correctness and
editorial correctness are different tests. A build can finish without
proving that a table is readable or a heading is useful.

\section{Equations and tables as separate languages}
\index{eqn}\index{tbl}\index{pic}\index{Cherry, Lorinda}

Mathematics is awkward to express using only primitive position changes.
Kernighan and Lorinda Cherry's eqn allowed an author to describe mathematical
relationships with words such as ``over,'' ``sup,'' and ``sqrt.'' The
preprocessor translated that description into lower-level formatting
instructions. The author worked closer to the structure of the expression
than to the motion of a typesetter.

Tables present a different problem. They need column descriptions,
alignment, rules, and repeating patterns. Michael Lesk's tbl supplied a
language for that domain. Later, pic offered a language for diagrams.
These programs were not merely collections of shortcuts. Each captured a
small body of knowledge and translated it into a form a more general
formatter could consume.

A schematic pipeline makes the organization clear:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={draw,rounded corners,font=\small,minimum height=9mm}]
\node (s) at (0,0) {Source};
\node (p) at (3,0) {Preprocessors};
\node (t) at (6.2,0) {troff};
\node (d) at (9,0) {Device output};
\draw[->] (s) -- (p);
\draw[->] (p) -- (t);
\draw[->] (t) -- (d);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

The ordering of actual preprocessors depends on the document. An equation
inside a table can constrain which transformation must happen first.
Composition is powerful because the stages communicate through agreed
representations; it is fragile when those agreements are misunderstood.
A pipeline is not automatically correct simply because every component
works separately.

The little-language approach also changes who can contribute. Someone who
understands tables can improve tbl without becoming an expert in disk
allocation. Someone who understands typography can improve macros without
rewriting the shell. The interface between programs becomes an organizational
boundary as well as a technical one.

\section{From one device to many}

The original troff was closely associated with the C/A/T. As other output
devices became attractive, hardwired assumptions became a cost.
Kernighan's device-independent troff separated formatting from a particular
machine's final output encoding. A backend could translate intermediate
output for a different typesetter or printer. This was a substantial
architectural change: it made the boundary between document layout and
device operation explicit.

Device independence is always qualified. Font metrics affect line breaking,
available glyphs affect notation, and device resolution affects appearance.
An intermediate language cannot make these differences disappear. It can
localize the knowledge needed to handle them. That is a more useful goal
than promising that every printer will produce identical pages.

Later implementations, especially groff, carried the tradition into free
software systems and new output formats. Manual pages kept the roff family
visible even when most users no longer prepared papers in troff. A command
such as \texttt{man} appears to present text, but its source tradition
includes semantic macros, formatters, pagers, terminal capabilities, and
installation conventions. The apparent simplicity is the result of a
pipeline of maintained agreements.

TeX and LaTeX belong beside this history without being troff descendants.
TeX pursued its own model of mathematical and typographic precision.
LaTeX emphasized document structure through a macro layer. Unix provided
an accommodating environment for such tools because files, command
execution, and redirection did not reserve publishing for one privileged
application. The book in the reader's hands uses LaTeX, while the title
acknowledges a different publishing lineage.

\section{What publishing taught the system}

Document preparation exercised Unix as a complete environment. It required
interactive editing, reliable files, stable tools, reproducible commands,
and output whose defects humans could see immediately. It rewarded the
ability to combine a specialized preprocessor with a general formatter.
It also exposed the cost of poorly defined interfaces: a change in a macro,
font table, or backend could alter a document far from the changed code.

That experience complicates the familiar slogan about doing one thing
well. Troff itself is a substantial interpreter. Macro packages can be
complex programs. A useful tool boundary need not produce a tiny program;
it should concentrate a coherent responsibility. Eqn's responsibility was
mathematical expression layout, not an arbitrary limit on source-code size.

Publishing also gave users reasons to preserve source. A photographic page
is an artifact; the editable file and its toolchain explain how that artifact
was made. Modern reproducible research and documentation builds extend the
same concern. When source, tools, fonts, and invocation are recorded, later
readers can revise the work instead of merely admiring the output.

The path from patent typists to technical publishing shows how a local need
can become a general method. Unix did not become useful because it insisted
that every problem was text. It became useful because text was a workable,
inspectable representation for many problems, and because the system let
users build new interpreters when ordinary text processing was insufficient.
